TypeScript Development Rules

Basic Principles
==============

- Use English for all code and documentation
- Always declare types for variables and functions
- Use JSDoc for public classes and methods
- Maintain single responsibility in functions
- Follow one export per file principle

Nomenclature
===========

- Use PascalCase for classes and types
- Use camelCase for variables and functions
- Use kebab-case for file names
- Use UPPERCASE for constants
- Start functions with verbs
- Use descriptive boolean names (isLoading, hasError)
- Avoid abbreviations except for standards (API, URL)

Type System Usage
===============

- Leverage interfaces for contracts
- Use type aliases for complex types
- Utilize enums for fixed sets
- Employ generics for reusability
- Use utility types (Partial, Required, Readonly)
- Apply strict null checks
- Use type inference appropriately
- Define custom types for complex data

Functions
========

- Keep functions short (< 20 lines)
- Use descriptive names with verbs
- Implement early returns
- Use higher-order functions
- Apply default parameters
- Use objects for multiple parameters
- Maintain single abstraction level

Data Handling
===========

- Use composite types over primitives
- Implement data validation in classes
- Prefer immutability
- Use readonly for constants
- Use as const for literals

Classes
======

- Follow SOLID principles
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Define clear interfaces
- Keep classes small and focused
- Limit public methods (< 10)
- Limit properties (< 10)

Error Handling
============

- Use exceptions for unexpected errors
- Implement proper error catching
- Add context to caught errors
- Use global handlers when appropriate

Testing
=======

- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert
- Use clear test variable names
- Write unit tests for public functions
- Implement acceptance tests
- Follow Given-When-Then convention

